[h1]Extra Build Layers[/h1]
More floors inside every platform, and a much taller stack of platforms in space.

Out of the box you get [b]17 more building floors[/b] inside every platform - 20 in all, as many as fit - and [b]20 more space levels[/b] above your current top one, both bought in the research shop like any other upgrade. Both counts are configurable.

[h2]What you get[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]More floors and more space levels[/b] - unlocked through new research shop entries, many levels per purchase.
[*][b]Tall lifts and pipes[/b] - drag a belt or pipe up or down any number of floors and it places one lift for the whole climb, drawn full height, with shapes riding it all the way.
[*][b]Lift stations for space[/b] - drag a space belt or pipe up or down any number of space levels and an entry and exit station are placed for you, joined by a shaft. Shapes take time to climb, at full belt speed. Click the shaft to select the lift, delete either end to remove both, and jump between the ends from the side panel.
[*][b]Getting around[/b] - hold the layer keys to keep moving, faster the longer you hold. Zoomed all the way out you stay on your layer and each press moves a big step. A [b]View[/b] button (key Z) beside Copy, Cut and Delete takes you straight to whatever you have selected.
[*][b]High layers stay visible[/b] - the game hides everything above its own top layer unless you look straight down. Not any more.
[*][b]Stands reach the floor[/b] - supports under belts, pipes and wires go all the way down, however high up they are.
[*][b]Numbered floors[/b] - every new platform floor and space layer is labelled with its own number, in the game's own style.
[*][b]A layer readout that scales[/b] - with many layers, the HUD shows the current layer's number instead of a stack of tiny handles.
[*][b]Placement previews and hover highlights that stay lit[/b] on the high floors.
[/list]

[h2]The one thing to know[/h2]
Only the [b]bottom three floors[/b] of a platform can carry ports to another platform. The game builds the transport between platforms for exactly three floors, and that cannot be changed from a mod. Everything inside a platform works on every floor - a tall factory just brings its output back down to export it.

Platforms are capped at [b]20 floors[/b] (0 to 19): the game stores a floor and its space level in one number, so a 21st floor would be the ground floor of the space level above. Space levels go up to [b]999[/b].

[h2]Save games[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Adding[/b] the mod to an existing save works - open it and the new layers appear in the shop.
[*][b]Removing[/b] it from a save you have played with it is refused by the game, as it should be: lifts and anything above floor 2 would break.
[*]Saves from earlier test versions with tall space lifts load with those lifts removed. Drag the climb again to place lift stations.
[*][b]Lowering[/b] a layer count on a save that already uses the higher layers is not safe. Raising one is fine.
[/list]

[h2]Configuration[/h2]
Copy [b]config.example.json[/b] to [b]config.json[/b] in the mod's folder. You can set how many floors and space levels to add, how they are unlocked (bought in the shop, granted from the start, or left to another mod), their prices, and how the HUD layer strip behaves. Every option is explained in the example file.

[h2]For mod authors[/h2]
This mod is built to be extended. A static [b]BuildLayers[/b] class lets your mod require a number of extra floors or space levels, replace how they are unlocked with your own policy, and find out what a session actually got. Buildings added by your mod work on the new floors with no extra work.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
Requires [b]Shapez Shifter[/b]. Other mods' buildings work on the new floors automatically.
